    common rule postulate 1.
        let
            P be an instance of plane and
            A be an instance of point and
            B be an instance of point and
        if
            A lies on P and
            B lies on P and
            A is not equal to B
        then
            it is possible
                draw AB as an instance of finite straight line the way
                    that AB lies on P and
                    AB is bounded by A, B.             
    postulate 2.
        let
            P be an instance of a plane and
            C be an instance of a point and
            D be an instance of a point and
            CD be an instance of finite straight line
                instantiated following postulate 1
                    with
                        P for P and
                        C for A and
                        D for B and
                        CD for AB
        if 
            C lies anywhere on P and
            D lies anywhere on P and
            CD is bounded by C,D
        then 
            it is possible 
                to extend CD up to point E creating the  finite straight line CE
                which is located on plane P and has boundaries C and E and have.

    postulate 3.
        let
            P be an instance of plane and
            C be an instance of point and
            R be an instance of point
        if
            C lies on P and
            R lies on P and
            C is not equal to R
        then
            it is possible
                draw circle CR as an instance of circle the way
                    that circle CR lies on P and
                    C is the center of circle CR and
                    R lies on the circumference of circle CR.
    postulate 4.
        let
            AAA be an instance of right angle and
            BBB be an instance of right angle
        then
            AAA is equal to BBB.
    postulate 5.
        let
            A be an instance of anything and
            B be an instance of anything
        if
            B is a part of A
        then
            A is greater than B.

    common rule common notion 1. Equality Transitivity.
        let
            A be an instance of anything and
            B be an instance of anything and
            C be an instance of anything
        if 
            A is equal to C and
            B is equal to C
        then
            A is equal to B.

    common rule common notion 3. Subtraction of Equals.
        let
            W1 be an instance of anything and
            W2 be an instance of anything and
            P1 be an instance of anything and
            P2 be an instance of anything
        if
            W1 is equal to W2 and
            P1 is equal to P2 and
            P1 is part of W1 and
            P2 is part of W2
        then
            remainder of W1 minus P1 is equal to remainder of W2 minus P2.


~------------ Implicit Common rules of Logic ------------~
    common rule condition of not equals being part of each other.
        let
            A be an instance of anything and
            B be an instance of anything
        if
            A is not equal to B and
            A lies on B 
        then 
            A is a part of B.
